{"id":"gen-1790968011-mpHuT3HQegA2j3jmpL5C","object":"chat.completion","created":1790968011,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5129-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 2,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 2,\n        \"e\": 1,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 0,\n        \"h\": 0\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5129\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5129\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve the CSP. The problem: we have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Ten constraints (c1..c10). Each constraint defines allowed pairs for a specific ordered pair of variables.\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [2,0], [2,1], [1,1], [2,2], [3,1].\nc2: (b,c) allowed: [0,3], [3,1], [2,2], [2,3], [2,0].\nc3: (c,d) allowed: [3,2], [0,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,0].\nc4: (d,a) allowed: [2,2], [3,2], [0,0], [3,3], [0,1].\nc5: (e,f) allowed: [1,0], [2,1], [0,2], [3,0], [0,1].\nc6: (f,g) allowed: [0,0], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3], [1,0].\nc7: (g,h) allowed: [0,0], [1,2], [2,1], [0,3], [3,1].\nc8: (h,e) allowed: [0,1], [1,0], [0,0], [2,1], [0,3].\nc9: (b,e) allowed: [0,1], [3,2].\nc10: (d,g) allowed: [2,0], [0,1].\n\nGoal: find a complete assignment for all eight variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to output JSON envelope with assignment values.\n\nWe need to find at least one solution. The problem also says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" But the instructions say \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we just need to output one assignment.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP.\n\nLet's solve manually.\n\nWe have a chain of constraints linking a,b,c,d in a cycle: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a. That's a 4-cycle. Then e,f,g,h cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. And cross constraints: b-e, d-g.\n\nWe need to find values 0-3.\n\nWe can attempt to brute force manually.\n\nLet's denote variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed: (2,0), (2,1), (1,1), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed: (0,3), (3,1), (2,2), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: (3,2), (0,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,0).\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed: (2,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,3), (0,1).\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: (1,0), (2,1), (0,2), (3,0), (0,1).\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: (0,0), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3), (1,0).\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: (0,0), (1,2), (2,1), (0,3), (3,1).\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: (0,1), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1), (0,3).\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed: (0,1), (3,2).\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed: (2,0), (0,1).\n\nGoal: assign.\n\nWe can approach systematically.\n\nFirst, note domain small, we can attempt to enumerate possibilities for a,b,c,d cycle.\n\nLet's treat variables a,b,c,d.\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) pairs: (2,0), (2,1), (1,1), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nc2: allowed (b,c) pairs: (0,3), (3,1), (2,2), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nc3: allowed (c,d) pairs: (3,2), (0,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,0).\n\nc4: allowed (d,a) pairs: (2,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,3), (0,1).\n\nWe need to satisfy all four constraints simultaneously.\n\nWe can try to search manually.\n\nOne approach: iterate over possible values for a (0-3) and see possible b from c1, then c from c2, then d from c3, then check c4.\n\nLet's list possible (a,b) pairs from c1:\n\n- a=2, b=0\n- a=2, b=1\n- a=1, b=1\n- a=2, b=2\n- a=3, b=1\n\nThus possible a values: 2 (with b=0,1,2), 1 (b=1), 3 (b=1). So a can be 2,1,3.\n\nNow for each a,b we need possible c via c2.\n\nc2: allowed (b,c) pairs: (0,3), (3,1), (2,2), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nThus given b we can list possible c:\n\n- If b=0: allowed c=3 (pair (0,3)). Also maybe other pairs? No, only (0,3). So c must be 3.\n\n- If b=1: allowed c: from (b,c) pairs: (3,1) means b=3? No, that's (3,1) with b=3, c=1. Not b=1. (2,2) b=2. (2,3) b=2. (2,0) b=2. So there is no (1,?) in c2 allowed pairs. So b=1 yields no allowed c. So b cannot be 1.\n\n- If b=2: allowed c: (2,2), (2,3), (2,0). So c can be 2,3,0.\n\n- If b=3: allowed c: (3,1). So c=1.\n\nThus b can be 0,2,3. b=1 is impossible.\n\nNow c1 also includes b possibilities: from c1, b can be 0,1,2 for a=2; b=1 for a=1; b=1 for a=3. So b=1 appears only for a=1 or a=3 or a=2? Actually c1 includes (2,1) and (3,1) and (1,1). So b=1 appears with a=2, a=1, a=3. But b=1 is not allowed for c2, so any assignment with b=1 is invalid. So we must eliminate those.\n\nThus b cannot be 1. So we must consider only c1 pairs where b !=1.\n\nList c1 pairs with b !=1:\n\n- (2,0) => a=2,b=0\n- (2,2) => a=2,b=2\n- (3,1) is b=1 => discard\n- (1,1) is b=1 => discard\n- (2,1) is b=1 => discard\n\nThus only possible (a,b) pairs: (2,0) and (2,2). So a must be 2, b either 0 or 2.\n\nNow consider each:\n\nCase 1: a=2,b=0.\n\nFrom c2: b=0 => c must be 3.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,2), (0,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,0). So c=3 => allowed d values? Look at pairs where c=3: (0,3) means c=0,d=3, not c=3. (3,2) means c=3,d=2. So d=2 is allowed. Also any other? No. So d must be 2.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed: (2,2) => d=2,a=2 allowed. Also (3,2) not relevant. (0,0) no. (3,3) no. (0,1) no. So (2,2) is allowed. So this assignment a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2 satisfies all four constraints.\n\nThus we have a consistent assignment for a,b,c,d: a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2.\n\nCheck all constraints: c1 (2,0) is allowed. c2 (0,3) is allowed. c3 (3,2) is allowed. c4 (2,2) is allowed. Good.\n\nCase 2: a=2,b=2.\n\nFrom c2: b=2 => c can be 2,3,0.\n\nWe need to consider each possible c.\n\n- Subcase 2a: c=2.\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: c=2 => allowed d: from pairs (2,1) and (2,0). So d can be 1 or 0.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed: a=2. Check allowed pairs (d,2) where d is 1 or 0.\n\nAllowed (d,a) pairs: (2,2) => d=2,a=2. (3,2) => d=3,a=2. (0,0) => d=0,a=0. (3,3) => d=3,a=3. (0,1) => d=0,a=1.\n\nThus (d,a) with a=2: allowed only if (d=2) from (2,2) or (d=3) from (3,2). So d must be 2 or 3. But our possible d values from c3 are 1 or 0. No match. So c=2 leads to no solution.\n\n- Subcase 2b: c=3.\n\nc3: c=3 => allowed d: from (0,3) pair c=0,d=3? No. (3,2) pair c=3,d=2. So d=2.\n\nc4: (d=2,a=2) allowed as before (2,2). So this works: a=2,b=2,c=3,d=2.\n\nCheck c1: (2,2) allowed. c2: (2,3) allowed. c3: (3,2) allowed. c4: (2,2) allowed.\n\nThus assignment a=2,b=2,c=3,d=2 works.\n\n- Subcase 2c: c=0.\n\nc3: c=0 => allowed d: from (0,3) and (0,0). So d can be 3 or 0.\n\nc4: (d,a) with a=2: allowed d must be 2 or 3. So d=3 works (since (3,2) allowed). d=0 does not work (0,2 not allowed). So d=3.\n\nThus assignment a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3.\n\nCheck constraints: c1 (2,2) allowed. c2 (2,0) allowed? c2 allowed pairs include (2,0). Yes. c3 (0,3) allowed? c3 includes (0,3). Yes. c4 (3,2) allowed? c4 includes (3,2). Yes.\n\nThus this is also a solution.\n\nThus we have three possible assignments for a,b,c,d:\n\n1) (a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2)\n2) (a=2,b=2,c=3,d=2)\n3) (a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3)\n\nWe need to also satisfy constraints for e,f,g,h and cross constraints b-e, d-g.\n\nNow we need to find e,f,g,h values.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: (1,0), (2,1), (0,2), (3,0), (0,1).\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: (0,0), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3), (1,0).\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: (0,0), (1,2), (2,1), (0,3), (3,1).\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: (0,1), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1), (0,3).\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed: (0,1), (3,2).\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed: (2,0), (0,1).\n\nWe have three possible (b,d) combos from above assignments:\n\nAssignment A: b=0, d=2.\nAssignment B: b=2, d=2.\nAssignment C: b=2, d=3.\n\nWe need to find e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints and cross constraints b-e and d-g.\n\nThus for each assignment, we need to find e,f,g,h.\n\nLet's analyze constraints for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe have a directed cycle: e->f->g->h->e.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: list all possibilities.\n\nLet's enumerate all allowed pairs for each variable pair.\n\nc5: allowed (e,f):\n- (1,0)\n- (2,1)\n- (0,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus possible (e,f):\n(e=1,f=0)\n(e=2,f=1)\n(e=0,f=2)\n(e=3,f=0)\n(e=0,f=1)\n\nSo e can be 0,1,2,3. For each e, possible f:\n\n- e=0: f can be 2 or 1 (two possibilities)\n- e=1: f=0\n- e=2: f=1\n- e=3: f=0\n\nThus e=0 has two f options, others have one.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed:\n- (0,0)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,3)\n- (1,0)\n\nThus possible (f,g):\n- f=0,g=0\n- f=2,g=0\n- f=0,g=3\n- f=1,g=3\n- f=1,g=0\n\nThus f can be 0,1,2. For each f:\n\n- f=0: g can be 0 or 3.\n- f=1: g can be 3 or 0.\n- f=2: g=0.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed:\n- (0,0)\n- (1,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,1)\n\nThus possible (g,h):\n- g=0,h=0\n- g=0,h=3\n- g=1,h=2\n- g=2,h=1\n- g=3,h=1\n\nThus g can be 0,1,2,3. For each g:\n\n- g=0: h=0 or 3.\n- g=1: h=2.\n- g=2: h=1.\n- g=3: h=1.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed:\n- (0,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (0,0)\n- (2,1)\n- (0,3)\n\nThus possible (h,e):\n- h=0,e=1\n- h=1,e=0\n- h=0,e=0\n- h=2,e=1\n- h=0,e=3\n\nThus h can be 0,1,2. For each h:\n\n- h=0: e can be 1,0,3 (multiple)\n- h=1: e=0\n- h=2: e=1\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed: (0,1) or (3,2). So given b, e must be one of these.\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed: (2,0) or (0,1). So given d, g must be one of these.\n\nThus we need to find e,f,g,h satisfying all constraints, plus b-e and d-g constraints.\n\nWe have three possible assignments for b,d. Let's explore each.\n\n### Assignment A: b=0, d=2.\n\nThen c9: (b=0,e) allowed: (0,1) only. So e must be 1.\n\nc10: (d=2,g) allowed: (2,0) only. So g must be 0.\n\nThus e=1, g=0.\n\nNow we need to find f and h consistent.\n\nWe have e=1. c5: (e,f) allowed: (1,0) only (since e=1 => f=0). So f must be 0.\n\nNow we have f=0. c6: (f,g) allowed: (0,0) or (0,3) (since f=0). g must be 0 per c10, and g=0 is allowed with f=0 (pair (0,0)). So consistent.\n\nNow we have g=0. c7: (g,h) allowed: (0,0) or (0,3). So h can be 0 or 3.\n\nNow we have h possibilities.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: e=1. So allowed (h,e) pairs: (0,1) allowed, (1,0) no because e=1, (0,0) no because e=0, (2,1) no because h=2, (0,3) no because e=3. So only (h=0,e=1) works. So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nCheck c7: (g=0,h=0) is allowed (0,0). Good.\n\nThus assignment for e,f,g,h: e=1,f=0,g=0,h=0.\n\nCheck all constraints:\n\nc5 (e=1,f=0) allowed: yes.\nc6 (f=0,g=0) allowed: yes.\nc7 (g=0,h=0) allowed: yes.\nc8 (h=0,e=1) allowed: yes.\nc9 (b=0,e=1) allowed: yes.\nc10 (d=2,g=0) allowed: yes.\n\nThus assignment A yields a complete solution:\n\na=2,b=0,c=3,d=2,e=1,f=0,g=0,h=0.\n\nWe need to verify c1-c4 already satisfied. Yes.\n\nThus we have a valid solution.\n\nWe should also consider other assignments for a,b,c,d (B and C) maybe also produce solutions, but we only need one. So we can output this solution.\n\nBut we need to ensure that the assignment is consistent with all constraints. Let's double-check all constraints:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (2,0) allowed: yes.\nc2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed: yes.\nc3 (c,d) = (3,2) allowed: yes.\nc4 (d,a) = (2,2) allowed: yes.\nc5 (e,f) = (1,0) allowed: yes.\nc6 (f,g) = (0,0) allowed: yes.\nc7 (g,h) = (0,0) allowed: yes.\nc8 (h,e) = (0,1) allowed: yes.\nc9 (b,e) = (0,1) allowed: yes.\nc10 (d,g) = (2,0) allowed: yes.\n\nAll good.\n\nThus we have a valid assignment.\n\nNow we need to output JSON envelope as per template.\n\nThe envelope template:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5129-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 0,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 0,\n        \"d\": 0,\n        \"e\": 0,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 0,\n        \"h\": 0\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5129\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5129\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nWe need to replace the placeholder assignment values with our solution values.\n\nThus assignment: a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2,e=1,f=0,g=0,h=0.\n\nWe need to ensure integers are JSON integers.\n\nThus final envelope:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5129-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 2,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 2,\n        \"e\": 1,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 0,\n        \"h\": 0\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5129\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5129\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nWe need to output raw JSON, no markdown, no extra text. Ensure proper formatting (commas). The envelope is exactly as required.\n\nThus output that JSON.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve the CSP. The problem: we have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Ten constraints (c1..c10). Each constraint defines allowed pairs for a specific ordered pair of variables.\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [2,0], [2,1], [1,1], [2,2], [3,1].\nc2: (b,c) allowed: [0,3], [3,1], [2,2], [2,3], [2,0].\nc3: (c,d) allowed: [3,2], [0,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,0].\nc4: (d,a) allowed: [2,2], [3,2], [0,0], [3,3], [0,1].\nc5: (e,f) allowed: [1,0], [2,1], [0,2], [3,0], [0,1].\nc6: (f,g) allowed: [0,0], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3], [1,0].\nc7: (g,h) allowed: [0,0], [1,2], [2,1], [0,3], [3,1].\nc8: (h,e) allowed: [0,1], [1,0], [0,0], [2,1], [0,3].\nc9: (b,e) allowed: [0,1], [3,2].\nc10: (d,g) allowed: [2,0], [0,1].\n\nGoal: find a complete assignment for all eight variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to output JSON envelope with assignment values.\n\nWe need to find at least one solution. The problem also says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" But the instructions say \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we just need to output one assignment.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP.\n\nLet's solve manually.\n\nWe have a chain of constraints linking a,b,c,d in a cycle: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a. That's a 4-cycle. Then e,f,g,h cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. And cross constraints: b-e, d-g.\n\nWe need to find values 0-3.\n\nWe can attempt to brute force manually.\n\nLet's denote variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed: (2,0), (2,1), (1,1), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed: (0,3), (3,1), (2,2), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: (3,2), (0,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,0).\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed: (2,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,3), (0,1).\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: (1,0), (2,1), (0,2), (3,0), (0,1).\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: (0,0), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3), (1,0).\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: (0,0), (1,2), (2,1), (0,3), (3,1).\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: (0,1), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1), (0,3).\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed: (0,1), (3,2).\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed: (2,0), (0,1).\n\nGoal: assign.\n\nWe can approach systematically.\n\nFirst, note domain small, we can attempt to enumerate possibilities for a,b,c,d cycle.\n\nLet's treat variables a,b,c,d.\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) pairs: (2,0), (2,1), (1,1), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nc2: allowed (b,c) pairs: (0,3), (3,1), (2,2), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nc3: allowed (c,d) pairs: (3,2), (0,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,0).\n\nc4: allowed (d,a) pairs: (2,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,3), (0,1).\n\nWe need to satisfy all four constraints simultaneously.\n\nWe can try to search manually.\n\nOne approach: iterate over possible values for a (0-3) and see possible b from c1, then c from c2, then d from c3, then check c4.\n\nLet's list possible (a,b) pairs from c1:\n\n- a=2, b=0\n- a=2, b=1\n- a=1, b=1\n- a=2, b=2\n- a=3, b=1\n\nThus possible a values: 2 (with b=0,1,2), 1 (b=1), 3 (b=1). So a can be 2,1,3.\n\nNow for each a,b we need possible c via c2.\n\nc2: allowed (b,c) pairs: (0,3), (3,1), (2,2), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nThus given b we can list possible c:\n\n- If b=0: allowed c=3 (pair (0,3)). Also maybe other pairs? No, only (0,3). So c must be 3.\n\n- If b=1: allowed c: from (b,c) pairs: (3,1) means b=3? No, that's (3,1) with b=3, c=1. Not b=1. (2,2) b=2. (2,3) b=2. (2,0) b=2. So there is no (1,?) in c2 allowed pairs. So b=1 yields no allowed c. So b cannot be 1.\n\n- If b=2: allowed c: (2,2), (2,3), (2,0). So c can be 2,3,0.\n\n- If b=3: allowed c: (3,1). So c=1.\n\nThus b can be 0,2,3. b=1 is impossible.\n\nNow c1 also includes b possibilities: from c1, b can be 0,1,2 for a=2; b=1 for a=1; b=1 for a=3. So b=1 appears only for a=1 or a=3 or a=2? Actually c1 includes (2,1) and (3,1) and (1,1). So b=1 appears with a=2, a=1, a=3. But b=1 is not allowed for c2, so any assignment with b=1 is invalid. So we must eliminate those.\n\nThus b cannot be 1. So we must consider only c1 pairs where b !=1.\n\nList c1 pairs with b !=1:\n\n- (2,0) => a=2,b=0\n- (2,2) => a=2,b=2\n- (3,1) is b=1 => discard\n- (1,1) is b=1 => discard\n- (2,1) is b=1 => discard\n\nThus only possible (a,b) pairs: (2,0) and (2,2). So a must be 2, b either 0 or 2.\n\nNow consider each:\n\nCase 1: a=2,b=0.\n\nFrom c2: b=0 => c must be 3.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,2), (0,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,0). So c=3 => allowed d values? Look at pairs where c=3: (0,3) means c=0,d=3, not c=3. (3,2) means c=3,d=2. So d=2 is allowed. Also any other? No. So d must be 2.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed: (2,2) => d=2,a=2 allowed. Also (3,2) not relevant. (0,0) no. (3,3) no. (0,1) no. So (2,2) is allowed. So this assignment a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2 satisfies all four constraints.\n\nThus we have a consistent assignment for a,b,c,d: a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2.\n\nCheck all constraints: c1 (2,0) is allowed. c2 (0,3) is allowed. c3 (3,2) is allowed. c4 (2,2) is allowed. Good.\n\nCase 2: a=2,b=2.\n\nFrom c2: b=2 => c can be 2,3,0.\n\nWe need to consider each possible c.\n\n- Subcase 2a: c=2.\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: c=2 => allowed d: from pairs (2,1) and (2,0). So d can be 1 or 0.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed: a=2. Check allowed pairs (d,2) where d is 1 or 0.\n\nAllowed (d,a) pairs: (2,2) => d=2,a=2. (3,2) => d=3,a=2. (0,0) => d=0,a=0. (3,3) => d=3,a=3. (0,1) => d=0,a=1.\n\nThus (d,a) with a=2: allowed only if (d=2) from (2,2) or (d=3) from (3,2). So d must be 2 or 3. But our possible d values from c3 are 1 or 0. No match. So c=2 leads to no solution.\n\n- Subcase 2b: c=3.\n\nc3: c=3 => allowed d: from (0,3) pair c=0,d=3? No. (3,2) pair c=3,d=2. So d=2.\n\nc4: (d=2,a=2) allowed as before (2,2). So this works: a=2,b=2,c=3,d=2.\n\nCheck c1: (2,2) allowed. c2: (2,3) allowed. c3: (3,2) allowed. c4: (2,2) allowed.\n\nThus assignment a=2,b=2,c=3,d=2 works.\n\n- Subcase 2c: c=0.\n\nc3: c=0 => allowed d: from (0,3) and (0,0). So d can be 3 or 0.\n\nc4: (d,a) with a=2: allowed d must be 2 or 3. So d=3 works (since (3,2) allowed). d=0 does not work (0,2 not allowed). So d=3.\n\nThus assignment a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3.\n\nCheck constraints: c1 (2,2) allowed. c2 (2,0) allowed? c2 allowed pairs include (2,0). Yes. c3 (0,3) allowed? c3 includes (0,3). Yes. c4 (3,2) allowed? c4 includes (3,2). Yes.\n\nThus this is also a solution.\n\nThus we have three possible assignments for a,b,c,d:\n\n1) (a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2)\n2) (a=2,b=2,c=3,d=2)\n3) (a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3)\n\nWe need to also satisfy constraints for e,f,g,h and cross constraints b-e, d-g.\n\nNow we need to find e,f,g,h values.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: (1,0), (2,1), (0,2), (3,0), (0,1).\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: (0,0), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3), (1,0).\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: (0,0), (1,2), (2,1), (0,3), (3,1).\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: (0,1), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1), (0,3).\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed: (0,1), (3,2).\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed: (2,0), (0,1).\n\nWe have three possible (b,d) combos from above assignments:\n\nAssignment A: b=0, d=2.\nAssignment B: b=2, d=2.\nAssignment C: b=2, d=3.\n\nWe need to find e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints and cross constraints b-e and d-g.\n\nThus for each assignment, we need to find e,f,g,h.\n\nLet's analyze constraints for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe have a directed cycle: e->f->g->h->e.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: list all possibilities.\n\nLet's enumerate all allowed pairs for each variable pair.\n\nc5: allowed (e,f):\n- (1,0)\n- (2,1)\n- (0,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus possible (e,f):\n(e=1,f=0)\n(e=2,f=1)\n(e=0,f=2)\n(e=3,f=0)\n(e=0,f=1)\n\nSo e can be 0,1,2,3. For each e, possible f:\n\n- e=0: f can be 2 or 1 (two possibilities)\n- e=1: f=0\n- e=2: f=1\n- e=3: f=0\n\nThus e=0 has two f options, others have one.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed:\n- (0,0)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,3)\n- (1,0)\n\nThus possible (f,g):\n- f=0,g=0\n- f=2,g=0\n- f=0,g=3\n- f=1,g=3\n- f=1,g=0\n\nThus f can be 0,1,2. For each f:\n\n- f=0: g can be 0 or 3.\n- f=1: g can be 3 or 0.\n- f=2: g=0.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed:\n- (0,0)\n- (1,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,1)\n\nThus possible (g,h):\n- g=0,h=0\n- g=0,h=3\n- g=1,h=2\n- g=2,h=1\n- g=3,h=1\n\nThus g can be 0,1,2,3. For each g:\n\n- g=0: h=0 or 3.\n- g=1: h=2.\n- g=2: h=1.\n- g=3: h=1.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed:\n- (0,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (0,0)\n- (2,1)\n- (0,3)\n\nThus possible (h,e):\n- h=0,e=1\n- h=1,e=0\n- h=0,e=0\n- h=2,e=1\n- h=0,e=3\n\nThus h can be 0,1,2. For each h:\n\n- h=0: e can be 1,0,3 (multiple)\n- h=1: e=0\n- h=2: e=1\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed: (0,1) or (3,2). So given b, e must be one of these.\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed: (2,0) or (0,1). So given d, g must be one of these.\n\nThus we need to find e,f,g,h satisfying all constraints, plus b-e and d-g constraints.\n\nWe have three possible assignments for b,d. Let's explore each.\n\n### Assignment A: b=0, d=2.\n\nThen c9: (b=0,e) allowed: (0,1) only. So e must be 1.\n\nc10: (d=2,g) allowed: (2,0) only. So g must be 0.\n\nThus e=1, g=0.\n\nNow we need to find f and h consistent.\n\nWe have e=1. c5: (e,f) allowed: (1,0) only (since e=1 => f=0). So f must be 0.\n\nNow we have f=0. c6: (f,g) allowed: (0,0) or (0,3) (since f=0). g must be 0 per c10, and g=0 is allowed with f=0 (pair (0,0)). So consistent.\n\nNow we have g=0. c7: (g,h) allowed: (0,0) or (0,3). So h can be 0 or 3.\n\nNow we have h possibilities.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: e=1. So allowed (h,e) pairs: (0,1) allowed, (1,0) no because e=1, (0,0) no because e=0, (2,1) no because h=2, (0,3) no because e=3. So only (h=0,e=1) works. So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nCheck c7: (g=0,h=0) is allowed (0,0). Good.\n\nThus assignment for e,f,g,h: e=1,f=0,g=0,h=0.\n\nCheck all constraints:\n\nc5 (e=1,f=0) allowed: yes.\nc6 (f=0,g=0) allowed: yes.\nc7 (g=0,h=0) allowed: yes.\nc8 (h=0,e=1) allowed: yes.\nc9 (b=0,e=1) allowed: yes.\nc10 (d=2,g=0) allowed: yes.\n\nThus assignment A yields a complete solution:\n\na=2,b=0,c=3,d=2,e=1,f=0,g=0,h=0.\n\nWe need to verify c1-c4 already satisfied. Yes.\n\nThus we have a valid solution.\n\nWe should also consider other assignments for a,b,c,d (B and C) maybe also produce solutions, but we only need one. So we can output this solution.\n\nBut we need to ensure that the assignment is consistent with all constraints. Let's double-check all constraints:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (2,0) allowed: yes.\nc2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed: yes.\nc3 (c,d) = (3,2) allowed: yes.\nc4 (d,a) = (2,2) allowed: yes.\nc5 (e,f) = (1,0) allowed: yes.\nc6 (f,g) = (0,0) allowed: yes.\nc7 (g,h) = (0,0) allowed: yes.\nc8 (h,e) = (0,1) allowed: yes.\nc9 (b,e) = (0,1) allowed: yes.\nc10 (d,g) = (2,0) allowed: yes.\n\nAll good.\n\nThus we have a valid assignment.\n\nNow we need to output JSON envelope as per template.\n\nThe envelope template:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5129-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 0,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 0,\n        \"d\": 0,\n        \"e\": 0,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 0,\n        \"h\": 0\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5129\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5129\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nWe need to replace the placeholder assignment values with our solution values.\n\nThus assignment: a=2,b=0,c=3,d=2,e=1,f=0,g=0,h=0.\n\nWe need to ensure integers are JSON integers.\n\nThus final envelope:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5129-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 2,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 2,\n        \"e\": 1,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 0,\n        \"h\": 0\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5129\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5129\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nWe need to output raw JSON, no markdown, no extra text. Ensure proper formatting (commas). The envelope is exactly as required.\n\nThus output that JSON.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1334,"completion_tokens":5081,"total_tokens":6415,"cost":0,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3006,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}